  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What to check first</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Ownership</strong>Documents written by contractors or partners may belong to someone else.</li>
        <li><strong>Confidential material</strong>Client names, pricing and private terms need removing or clearance before any review.</li>
        <li><strong>Version history</strong>Earlier versions add context, but only if you hold the right to share them.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What a document set teaches a model</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A finished document records the rule. Its revisions record the thinking: what the first version assumed, what went wrong, and why the rule was rewritten. That reasoning is what a final PDF hides.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>What changed between versions</strong>The clause that was added, removed or reworded, and in which order.</li>
        <li><strong>Why it changed</strong>Comments, review notes and tracked changes that state the reason rather than the edit.</li>
        <li><strong>What the earlier version assumed</strong>The process or constraint the document was written against, before it was updated.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What keeps documents out of scope</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Ownership is the usual obstacle here, not sensitivity. A document can sit in your own drive and still belong to somebody else.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Deliverables owned by the client</strong>Work product an engagement letter assigns to the client cannot be licensed by you.</li>
        <li><strong>Material under a third-party NDA</strong>Anything received under confidentiality stays bound by it, whatever folder it lives in.</li>
        <li><strong>Contractor drafts without an assignment</strong>Material written by an agency or contractor needs a written assignment before it can be included.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

